# Line endings are normalised in the repository and checked out as LF everywhere.
#
# Why this file exists: several tests read their own source files and assert on
# the shape of the code. Node's `readFileSync(..., "utf-8")` returns the bytes as
# they are, so on a CRLF checkout an anchor like `indexOf("\n}\n")` returns -1 —
# and -1 fed to `slice(start, -1)` does not fail, it silently widens the region
# to almost the whole file. The assertion then measures the wrong text and its
# result is accidental. Found 2026-09-12 in `detail-panel-matrix.test.tsx`.
#
# ⛔ This file alone is not the fix. It governs future checkouts; the assertion
# itself was made newline-agnostic in `frontend/src/__tests__/sliceSource.ts`,
# which throws rather than returning a wrong slice. Both halves are needed —
# a clone made before this file existed still has whatever endings it has.
#
# (Python is immune by construction: its text mode translates CRLF to LF before
# the test ever sees it. Only the TypeScript side was exposed.)
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts and units are executed by Linux — CRLF in a shebang line breaks
# them with a confusing "bad interpreter" error.
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer   text eol=lf

# Vitest snapshots are compared as strings; a stray \r makes every one of them
# differ on a fresh clone.
*.snap text eol=lf

# ⛔ Binary assets: NEVER normalise. `text=auto` guesses from content, and a
# wrong guess here does not fail loudly — it rewrites bytes inside test fixtures
# and the parser blames the data. These are real scientific formats, not
# placeholders: NetCDF grids, NumPy archives, a GeoTIFF and a full ESRI
# shapefile set, all read by tests under backend/tests/fixtures/.
*.nc   binary
*.npz  binary
*.npy  binary
*.tif  binary
*.tiff binary
*.shp  binary
*.shx  binary
*.dbf  binary
*.png  binary
*.webp binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# ⚠️ Deliberately NOT binary: `.prj` (shapefile projection) is plain WKT text,
# and `.tab` (PANGAEA export) is tab-separated text. Marking text as binary
# would hide every future diff on them.

# ⛔ Provider CSV exports keep the bytes the provider sent, including CRLF.
# Measured 2026-09-12 with `git ls-files --eol`: two fixtures are stored CRLF in
# both the index and the working tree —
#   backend/tests/fixtures/arctic_rivers/arcticgro_ob_real.csv   (ArcticGRO)
#   backend/tests/fixtures/geotraces/seawater_real_subset.csv    (GEOTRACES)
# They are captured evidence of what the upstream file actually looks like, not
# source we author. Under the `* text=auto eol=lf` rule above git would rewrite
# their line endings on the next `git add`, quietly altering a recorded artefact
# so that it no longer matches what the provider serves. `-text` means "store
# and check out these bytes exactly".
#
# Nothing breaks by keeping them: Python's text mode translates CRLF to LF on
# read, and `csv` handles either ending, so the parsers never see the difference.
backend/tests/fixtures/**/*.csv -text
